Those lips that Love's own hand did make
Breathed forth the sound that said "I hate"
To me that languished for her sake;
And, fearing they might be false,
They did not publish it till she died.
And yet they had the grace to publish
That sweet, sweet love that I loved so;
And, despite of all their lies,
They were true in their love, and true in their telling.
  But, lo, I will prove them false, and prove
  That sweet love that I loved so well.


Poor soul, the center of my sinful earth,
Pressed with these rebel powers that thee array,
Why dost thou pine within and suffer dearth,
When I, full of all thy sweetest thoughts, am full of all thy
Thyself?
Why do I dwell upon thy beauty, and not thy worth,
Or what thou dost bestow in me,
That I may bestow what I bestow in thee?
O, be contented, my love, be contented with this:
I have more than enough of thee, and that is all.
  Save, that I have but a small store, and that is all.


My love is as a fever, longing still
For that which longer nurseth the disease,
Feeding on that which doth preserve the ill,
And that which in health doth make well.
Love's fever cures not the sick, but it cures the well;
And if it cure not the sick, it cures love;
For if it do not cure love, then it is but a fever
That heals not the sick, but heals the world.
  But if love be sick, and it be cured
  By this simple medicine, which is this,
  "Love is as a fever, longing still to be well."


