The little love-god, lying once asleep,
Laid by his side his heart-inflaming brand,
Whilst many nymphs that vowed chaste life to keep
Were dumb with wonder at his beauty,
And, like drowsing birds, slept on the ground;
And in dreams the sweetest song
Of his lovely voice was played,
And sweetest flowers were seen in his brow,
And in his sleep all was fair,
And all the world seemed fair to the eye.
Then did the king's fair-haired son
Return from the fairies' play, and, praising the sight,
Described their beauty with such praise
That the fairies' tears seemed but a sweet mist,
And the king's fair returned again to sleep.



When my love swears that she is made of truth,
I do believe her, though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Whose ignorance of true learning is no whit to be praised;
But she loves that which she knows to be true,
And therefore, though she be deceived, she is not deceived
By that which she conceives to be false.
For she that she deceives is not deceived
By what she conceives to be true,
But is deceived in what she finds true.
  And this is why I love her so much,
  That she tells me what she thinks of my deeds,
  And I do not think of her but what I say.


Two loves I have, of comfort and despair,
That like two spirits do suggest me still.
My better angel is a man right fair,
Whose gentle aspect I find so,
Whose gentle aspect I see not so,
Because that his gentle aspect is false.
For what beauty doth he bring to his face
Which alters not his shape,
Or alters but his complexion to look like it?
Or what sweet odor doth he doth change
That in his perfumed air I smell
When sweet perfumes are mixed with odors of hell,
  Wherein sweet perfumes are mixed with sweet odors of heaven?
  I love him not for his beauty,
  But for his beauty's perfumes.


